WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.500 align:middle line:90%


00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.000 align:middle line:90%
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.000 --> 00:01:43.000 align:middle line:90%


00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:43.500 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:50.490 align:middle line:84%
I hope you all
had a good break--

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:55.860 align:middle line:84%
spring break, as they
call it, uncreatively.

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.318 align:middle line:90%
I certainly had one.

00:01:57.318 --> 00:01:58.110 align:middle line:90%
Didn't do any work.

00:01:58.110 --> 00:01:58.777 align:middle line:90%
That's for sure.

00:01:58.777 --> 00:02:02.340 align:middle line:90%


00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:06.000 align:middle line:84%
Why would they put
this philosophy class

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.639 align:middle line:90%
in an engineering building?

00:02:08.639 --> 00:02:11.500 align:middle line:90%
Hold on one moment, please.

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:15.270 align:middle line:84%
I'm speaking, and I will get
to you when it's your turn.

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:23.070 align:middle line:90%
I am Professor Breck Knowles.

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:26.130 align:middle line:90%
This is Advanced Ethics.

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:29.505 align:middle line:84%
You should be upper-class
philosophy majors.

00:02:29.505 --> 00:02:32.070 align:middle line:90%


00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:35.130 align:middle line:84%
That should be
all right in here.

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:37.500 align:middle line:90%
Can you please wait your turn?

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:38.000 align:middle line:90%
Please.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.710 align:middle line:90%


00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:44.410 align:middle line:90%
This is the textbook.

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:48.630 align:middle line:84%
Now, this textbook covers some
of the most important topics

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:52.680 align:middle line:84%
across ethics, edited, of
course, by yours truly.

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:54.420 align:middle line:90%
[CHUCKLES]

00:02:54.420 --> 00:03:00.720 align:middle line:84%
I expect you all to have
it, and you can pull that--

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.965 align:middle line:90%
none of you have it.

00:03:03.965 --> 00:03:10.100 align:middle line:84%
[SIGHS] The bookstore must have
really messed up this time.

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:10.820 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:15.410 align:middle line:84%
When you do get it, make sure
you get the new latest edition.

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:20.150 align:middle line:84%
I don't get my cut
for used books.

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:27.350 align:middle line:84%
Now, if you turn the page
432, that's my chapter.

00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:28.040 align:middle line:90%
Could you read?

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:31.246 align:middle line:90%


00:03:31.246 --> 00:03:34.390 align:middle line:84%
[CLEARS THROAT]
Chapter 18, "Bias

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:36.460 align:middle line:90%
is Bad," by Brack Know-less.

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:37.755 align:middle line:90%
Knowles!

00:03:37.755 --> 00:03:39.917 align:middle line:90%
It's Knowles.

00:03:39.917 --> 00:03:44.500 align:middle line:84%
[SIGHS] You surely don't know
how to impress your professors.

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:47.470 align:middle line:90%
You read.

00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:51.100 align:middle line:84%
When investigating the
societal implications of bias

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:54.460 align:middle line:84%
within the framework of
non-hierarchical dependencies,

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:57.910 align:middle line:84%
we must integrate that the
taxonomy of bias and inequity

00:03:57.910 --> 00:04:00.250 align:middle line:90%
are combinationally intertwined.

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:05.350 align:middle line:84%
First and foremost, bias affects
both the sub- super-conscious--

00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:07.060 align:middle line:90%
Apologies for being late.

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:08.692 align:middle line:84%
Computer systems
ask me to help fix

00:04:08.692 --> 00:04:10.150 align:middle line:84%
a glitch that
happened this morning

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:13.480 align:middle line:84%
and affected which classrooms
a few professors are sent to.

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:14.260 align:middle line:90%
Hi.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:15.330 align:middle line:90%
I'm Sophie Muller.

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:16.885 align:middle line:90%
I don't think we've met.

00:04:16.885 --> 00:04:19.510 align:middle line:84%
That's what I was trying to tell
Professor Know-less, that he's

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:22.360 align:middle line:90%
in the wrong classroom.

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:24.010 align:middle line:90%
My apologies.

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:26.170 align:middle line:84%
The problem with the software
upgrade this morning,

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:27.790 align:middle line:90%
but it's all fixed.

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:29.650 align:middle line:90%
This is not the advanced class?

00:04:29.650 --> 00:04:30.430 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:32.050 align:middle line:84%
That's what I was
trying to tell you.

00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:34.360 align:middle line:90%
We are in engineering ethics.

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:40.159 align:middle line:84%
Well, I always am supposed
to teach the advanced class.

00:04:40.159 --> 00:04:44.080 align:middle line:84%
I don't want to be
stuck with beginners.

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:46.540 align:middle line:90%
But where am I supposed to go?

00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:47.260 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:49.960 align:middle line:90%
Knowles?

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:52.840 align:middle line:84%
Your class is all of the way
back in the philosophy floor

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:56.680 align:middle line:84%
in the subbasement of
building seven, room 001.

00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:57.909 align:middle line:90%
A bit of a hike, I'm afraid.

00:04:57.909 --> 00:05:01.990 align:middle line:84%
[CHUCKLES] Always get
stuck in the cave.

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:04.910 align:middle line:90%


00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:06.260 align:middle line:90%
Excuse me.

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:09.770 align:middle line:90%
Do you want your book back?

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:11.280 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:15.140 align:middle line:84%
You engineers can make something
that US philosophers deserve.

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:16.047 align:middle line:90%
Just--

00:05:16.047 --> 00:05:17.982 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:17.982 --> 00:05:18.482 align:middle line:90%


00:05:18.482 --> 00:05:21.891 align:middle line:90%
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:21.891 --> 00:05:25.300 align:middle line:90%


00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:27.530 align:middle line:90%
How is everyone doing?

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:28.030 align:middle line:90%
Oh.

00:05:28.030 --> 00:05:30.380 align:middle line:84%
I guess I didn't introduce
myself to all of you.

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:31.600 align:middle line:90%
I'm Sophie Muller.

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:33.450 align:middle line:84%
I'll be taking over
from Professor Baer, who

00:05:33.450 --> 00:05:35.200 align:middle line:84%
has been asked to run
an important project

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:37.270 align:middle line:90%
on the ethics of AI.

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:38.950 align:middle line:84%
Did you have a
good spring break?

00:05:38.950 --> 00:05:41.110 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:43.420 align:middle line:84%
That's good, but the
registration list

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:44.740 align:middle line:90%
said 40 students.

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:47.450 align:middle line:90%
Where is everyone else?

00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:47.950 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:05:47.950 --> 00:05:50.590 align:middle line:84%
Well, the others went to
go hear the president speak

00:05:50.590 --> 00:05:53.380 align:middle line:84%
about the new administrative
organizational structure.

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:55.270 align:middle line:90%
He's changed it again.

00:05:55.270 --> 00:05:56.620 align:middle line:90%
Third time this year.

00:05:56.620 --> 00:05:57.580 align:middle line:90%
Mhm.

00:05:57.580 --> 00:05:59.560 align:middle line:90%
The university president.

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:00.670 align:middle line:90%
Sounds interesting.

00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:01.930 align:middle line:90%
[CHUCKLING]

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:04.180 align:middle line:84%
But I thought students almost
never go to such things.

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:04.810 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:07.392 align:middle line:84%
But since no one goes to
these and the president

00:06:07.392 --> 00:06:09.850 align:middle line:84%
didn't want to speak in front
of an empty auditorium again,

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:12.430 align:middle line:84%
he offered everyone
free food and drink

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:14.200 align:middle line:84%
and allowed them
to break the rules

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:15.910 align:middle line:84%
and eat and drink
in the auditorium.

00:06:15.910 --> 00:06:18.460 align:middle line:84%
He also hired a large
steam cleaning crew,

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:20.380 align:middle line:84%
complete with industrial
drying equipment

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:22.090 align:middle line:90%
to clean up right afterwards.

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:23.390 align:middle line:90%
I see.

00:06:23.390 --> 00:06:23.890 align:middle line:90%
Oh.

00:06:23.890 --> 00:06:25.540 align:middle line:84%
I realized that I
introduced myself

00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:27.940 align:middle line:84%
but got distracted
by our conversation

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:29.860 align:middle line:84%
and didn't have you
introduce yourselves.

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:31.510 align:middle line:90%
What are your names?

00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:32.440 align:middle line:90%
Alyssa.

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:33.400 align:middle line:90%
I'm Patch.

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:34.420 align:middle line:90%
My name is Sophie.

00:06:34.420 --> 00:06:35.000 align:middle line:90%
Alvis.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:35.500 align:middle line:90%
Glenda.

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:36.370 align:middle line:90%
Pleased to meet you.

00:06:36.370 --> 00:06:38.770 align:middle line:84%
Well, nice to meet
all of you too.

00:06:38.770 --> 00:06:41.770 align:middle line:84%
So, you were telling me about
the event going on right now.

00:06:41.770 --> 00:06:45.610 align:middle line:90%
But why aren't you five there?

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:46.630 align:middle line:90%
Yes, Sophie.

00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:49.150 align:middle line:84%
Well, professor, don't
tell anyone else,

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:52.840 align:middle line:84%
but we're really
dedicated to understanding

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:55.850 align:middle line:84%
the ethical issues
of bias in AI.

00:06:55.850 --> 00:06:58.030 align:middle line:84%
In fact, instead of
traveling over spring break,

00:06:58.030 --> 00:07:00.760 align:middle line:84%
we just stayed here to
discuss ethics altogether.

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:06.520 align:middle line:84%
And we looked at the resources
that we could find online.

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:08.080 align:middle line:90%
Your dedication is admirable.

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:10.460 align:middle line:84%
What did you find
in your research?

00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:13.090 align:middle line:84%
Well, there are a bunch
of videos and articles

00:07:13.090 --> 00:07:15.400 align:middle line:90%
online, but--

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:16.150 align:middle line:90%
But what?

00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:20.020 align:middle line:90%
We found them unsatisfying.

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:22.110 align:middle line:90%
Why is that?

00:07:22.110 --> 00:07:22.610 align:middle line:90%
Alyssa.

00:07:22.610 --> 00:07:25.510 align:middle line:84%
Well, not everyone
found them unsatisfying.

00:07:25.510 --> 00:07:27.100 align:middle line:84%
I thought they made
some good points.

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:29.950 align:middle line:84%
Well, you weren't able to
explain away their failings.

00:07:29.950 --> 00:07:30.550 align:middle line:90%
Were you?

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:32.710 align:middle line:84%
No, but you weren't able
to give a better approach.

00:07:32.710 --> 00:07:33.490 align:middle line:90%
Were you?

00:07:33.490 --> 00:07:34.510 align:middle line:90%
Hold on, you two.

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:35.440 align:middle line:90%
Always bickering.

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:37.780 align:middle line:84%
We didn't answer
professor's question.

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:41.260 align:middle line:84%
Professor Muller, we found
them unsatisfying because they

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:42.820 align:middle line:90%
didn't give us an answer.

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:43.480 align:middle line:90%
That's right.

00:07:43.480 --> 00:07:45.110 align:middle line:84%
They told us that
it's a problem,

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:46.930 align:middle line:84%
and they said that
it's a hard problem,

00:07:46.930 --> 00:07:50.030 align:middle line:84%
but they didn't tell
us how to address it.

00:07:50.030 --> 00:07:51.340 align:middle line:90%
It's more than that.

00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:54.620 align:middle line:84%
The videos on bias in
AI are all the same.

00:07:54.620 --> 00:07:57.190 align:middle line:84%
They all say it's in
the data set-- bias in,

00:07:57.190 --> 00:08:00.460 align:middle line:84%
bias out-- and they keep
recycling the same examples.

00:08:00.460 --> 00:08:03.430 align:middle line:84%
Some videos have better
graphics or better speakers,

00:08:03.430 --> 00:08:05.470 align:middle line:84%
but other than that,
they're all the same.

00:08:05.470 --> 00:08:08.120 align:middle line:90%
[SCOFFS] So conformist.

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:08.620 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:11.440 align:middle line:84%
And while they say that
bias is in the data set,

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:13.850 align:middle line:84%
there seems to be
no general solution.

00:08:13.850 --> 00:08:16.930 align:middle line:84%
They just indicate that for
each algorithm the issue of bias

00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:19.060 align:middle line:90%
needs to be researched.

00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:22.510 align:middle line:84%
Even for that, they don't
give a systematic approach.

00:08:22.510 --> 00:08:23.140 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:25.240 align:middle line:84%
Even if you could choose
the right data set

00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:28.030 align:middle line:84%
and quantify it on
that data set to ensure

00:08:28.030 --> 00:08:30.790 align:middle line:84%
no bias in your training
set for a given analysis,

00:08:30.790 --> 00:08:34.510 align:middle line:84%
you have no way to know if
that applies to all situations.

00:08:34.510 --> 00:08:37.510 align:middle line:84%
My point is that we all
agree that bias is bad.

00:08:37.510 --> 00:08:40.450 align:middle line:84%
We simply need to address
it in every single algorithm

00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:41.470 align:middle line:90%
that we make.

00:08:41.470 --> 00:08:42.980 align:middle line:90%
Yes, I agree.

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:44.800 align:middle line:84%
We don't want any
bias in our AI.

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:46.640 align:middle line:90%
We want everyone to be happy.

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:48.640 align:middle line:84%
This is a good start,
but perhaps it's

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:50.470 align:middle line:90%
best if we get to specifics.

00:08:50.470 --> 00:08:53.290 align:middle line:84%
Alyssa, what were some of
the good points regarding

00:08:53.290 --> 00:08:55.300 align:middle line:84%
algorithmic bias
that you mentioned?

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:57.400 align:middle line:84%
For example,
algorithms are biased

00:08:57.400 --> 00:08:59.650 align:middle line:84%
against underrepresented
minorities.

00:08:59.650 --> 00:09:01.840 align:middle line:84%
Facial recognition doesn't
work as well for Blacks

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:04.480 align:middle line:84%
as it does for Whites,
especially Black women.

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:06.760 align:middle line:84%
One common example shows
that while it identifies

00:09:06.760 --> 00:09:09.910 align:middle line:84%
White males nearly perfectly,
it misidentified Black woman

00:09:09.910 --> 00:09:11.980 align:middle line:90%
over 20% of the time.

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:16.480 align:middle line:84%
Well, one algorithm gives
20% error and the other 30%.

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:19.270 align:middle line:84%
They can't even be
consistent in their bias.

00:09:19.270 --> 00:09:22.000 align:middle line:84%
If we can't nail down the
extent of the problem,

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:23.215 align:middle line:90%
how do we fix it?

00:09:23.215 --> 00:09:25.590 align:middle line:84%
You could at least work to
try to make sure that the data

00:09:25.590 --> 00:09:27.160 align:middle line:90%
set is more representative.

00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:30.910 align:middle line:84%
Oh, and what about the algorithm
that identifies Indian brides

00:09:30.910 --> 00:09:31.960 align:middle line:90%
as actors?

00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:34.030 align:middle line:90%
Well, why should they complain?

00:09:34.030 --> 00:09:37.630 align:middle line:84%
Being identified as an actor
is the greatest honor anyone

00:09:37.630 --> 00:09:38.290 align:middle line:90%
can have.

00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:39.710 align:middle line:90%
Oh, very funny.

00:09:39.710 --> 00:09:41.230 align:middle line:90%
I like chicken sandwiches.

00:09:41.230 --> 00:09:43.450 align:middle line:84%
How would you like it if
an algorithm identified you

00:09:43.450 --> 00:09:44.570 align:middle line:90%
as a chicken sandwich?

00:09:44.570 --> 00:09:45.070 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:09:45.070 --> 00:09:48.190 align:middle line:84%
I see why they call it
an adversarial example.

00:09:48.190 --> 00:09:50.800 align:middle line:84%
In any case, it's not
easy to address bias

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:52.480 align:middle line:84%
across the board
because of skews

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:54.880 align:middle line:84%
in data sets due to
historical inequities.

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:56.260 align:middle line:90%
And those skews differ.

00:09:56.260 --> 00:09:58.990 align:middle line:84%
At least we know that there
have been historical inequities

00:09:58.990 --> 00:10:00.500 align:middle line:90%
that we need to address.

00:10:00.500 --> 00:10:03.310 align:middle line:84%
And while it may
not be easy to do,

00:10:03.310 --> 00:10:07.360 align:middle line:84%
we have to at least try, even
if it's on a case-by-case basis.

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:09.520 align:middle line:84%
Even if it is on a
case-by-case basis,

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:11.200 align:middle line:84%
we're doomed to
miss a lot of bias

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:13.180 align:middle line:84%
until long after
everyone is equal.

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:15.910 align:middle line:84%
And I for one am
not that patient.

00:10:15.910 --> 00:10:18.370 align:middle line:84%
Well, this problem
is so important

00:10:18.370 --> 00:10:21.370 align:middle line:84%
that even if it takes a very
long time and even if we

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:23.170 align:middle line:84%
have to do it
algorithm by algorithm,

00:10:23.170 --> 00:10:24.700 align:middle line:90%
that is what we should do.

00:10:24.700 --> 00:10:27.410 align:middle line:84%
If we all do this,
everyone will be happy.

00:10:27.410 --> 00:10:29.230 align:middle line:84%
But no, many people
won't be happy

00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:31.460 align:middle line:90%
waiting for the bias to go away.

00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:35.050 align:middle line:84%
So, professor, this is where
we got stuck over spring break.

00:10:35.050 --> 00:10:37.060 align:middle line:84%
It's just not
practical to engineer

00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:39.220 align:middle line:90%
every algorithm against bias.

00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:41.710 align:middle line:84%
Even if we could, we would
end up eliminating it

00:10:41.710 --> 00:10:43.000 align:middle line:90%
in a few dimensions.

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:46.390 align:middle line:84%
But then we're bound to miss it
in other important dimensions.

00:10:46.390 --> 00:10:48.490 align:middle line:84%
Above and beyond
that, we can't even

00:10:48.490 --> 00:10:52.000 align:middle line:84%
quantify the bias consistently
since different studies

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:55.660 align:middle line:84%
on the same data set give
different amounts of bias.

00:10:55.660 --> 00:10:58.210 align:middle line:84%
We know that we need to do
something to address bias,

00:10:58.210 --> 00:11:02.425 align:middle line:84%
but what the videos say will at
best take a small step forward.

00:11:02.425 --> 00:11:04.300 align:middle line:84%
We know that others may
be content with that,

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:07.900 align:middle line:84%
but it's not sufficient for us
to feel good about ourselves.

00:11:07.900 --> 00:11:09.080 align:middle line:90%
No, it's not.

00:11:09.080 --> 00:11:09.580 align:middle line:90%
Ah.

00:11:09.580 --> 00:11:12.710 align:middle line:84%
You have reached an impasse,
which, believe it or not,

00:11:12.710 --> 00:11:16.660 align:middle line:84%
is often a necessary place to
be before you can make progress.

00:11:16.660 --> 00:11:19.930 align:middle line:84%
I think, however, it's best if
we take a step back and explore

00:11:19.930 --> 00:11:22.690 align:middle line:84%
how these algorithms work
and the roots of wanting

00:11:22.690 --> 00:11:23.980 align:middle line:90%
to eliminate bias.

00:11:23.980 --> 00:11:27.382 align:middle line:90%
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:11:27.382 --> 00:11:35.660 align:middle line:90%


00:11:35.660 --> 00:11:39.080 align:middle line:84%
So you probably all know
this, but just to remind you,

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:41.272 align:middle line:90%
the overall approach is simple.

00:11:41.272 --> 00:11:42.980 align:middle line:84%
You start out with a
training set of data

00:11:42.980 --> 00:11:46.700 align:middle line:84%
here together with descriptors,
which we use to derive a model.

00:11:46.700 --> 00:11:49.490 align:middle line:84%
Then we input the data,
and the model classifies it

00:11:49.490 --> 00:11:51.200 align:middle line:90%
based on some criterion.

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:53.750 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, the devil
is in the details.

00:11:53.750 --> 00:11:55.520 align:middle line:84%
Based on this
high-level approach,

00:11:55.520 --> 00:11:58.420 align:middle line:90%
where do you see bias?

00:11:58.420 --> 00:12:00.250 align:middle line:90%
There's no bias here.

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:03.520 align:middle line:84%
The algorithm-- say, a
convolutional neural network--

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:06.820 align:middle line:84%
objectively analyzes
the data provided to it.

00:12:06.820 --> 00:12:07.720 align:middle line:90%
It's just math.

00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:09.280 align:middle line:90%
It is not just math.

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:10.930 align:middle line:90%
We choose the training set.

00:12:10.930 --> 00:12:13.060 align:middle line:90%
We choose the algorithm.

00:12:13.060 --> 00:12:14.920 align:middle line:84%
Even if we think that
we are not biased,

00:12:14.920 --> 00:12:17.080 align:middle line:90%
we are subconsciously biased.

00:12:17.080 --> 00:12:19.150 align:middle line:84%
How do we actually
choose them if everything

00:12:19.150 --> 00:12:21.100 align:middle line:90%
is described by mathematics?

00:12:21.100 --> 00:12:23.350 align:middle line:90%
We don't really have a choice.

00:12:23.350 --> 00:12:24.040 align:middle line:90%
Duh.

00:12:24.040 --> 00:12:27.670 align:middle line:84%
The bias is already in the data
due to historical inequity.

00:12:27.670 --> 00:12:28.990 align:middle line:90%
You said it yourself.

00:12:28.990 --> 00:12:31.420 align:middle line:84%
So then our choice is
to note that and add

00:12:31.420 --> 00:12:33.220 align:middle line:90%
an appropriate loss function.

00:12:33.220 --> 00:12:35.360 align:middle line:84%
Does everybody know
what a loss function is?

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:36.780 align:middle line:90%
Of course.

00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:37.320 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:37.950 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:12:37.950 --> 00:12:40.110 align:middle line:84%
A loss function is
just the error function

00:12:40.110 --> 00:12:42.990 align:middle line:84%
associated with an event,
such as facial recognition.

00:12:42.990 --> 00:12:45.060 align:middle line:84%
It is what should be
mathematically minimized.

00:12:45.060 --> 00:12:46.200 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:12:46.200 --> 00:12:49.350 align:middle line:84%
Just to clarify, it
shouldn't be simply minimized

00:12:49.350 --> 00:12:52.510 align:middle line:84%
across the board but
targeted, for example,

00:12:52.510 --> 00:12:54.900 align:middle line:84%
so that the overall error
in facial recognition

00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:56.700 align:middle line:84%
is minimized with the
constraint that it is

00:12:56.700 --> 00:12:58.600 align:middle line:90%
equal among different groups.

00:12:58.600 --> 00:13:00.720 align:middle line:84%
But unless you have
equivalent training sets

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:03.090 align:middle line:84%
for the different groups,
the statistical errors

00:13:03.090 --> 00:13:04.230 align:middle line:90%
won't be equivalent.

00:13:04.230 --> 00:13:06.570 align:middle line:84%
And you can't quantify
the errors in advance

00:13:06.570 --> 00:13:09.220 align:middle line:84%
to determine whether or not
they will be equivalent.

00:13:09.220 --> 00:13:12.300 align:middle line:84%
In other words, you can't choose
the loss function approach

00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:16.020 align:middle line:84%
to solve the problem because
the fluctuations or randomness.

00:13:16.020 --> 00:13:18.270 align:middle line:90%
The problem is statistics.

00:13:18.270 --> 00:13:20.490 align:middle line:84%
We have to have
a very large data

00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:23.820 align:middle line:84%
set to average out randomness
to acceptably small percentages

00:13:23.820 --> 00:13:27.220 align:middle line:84%
and make sure that our
data is representative.

00:13:27.220 --> 00:13:30.390 align:middle line:84%
Yes, but my point is that
it's just not possible.

00:13:30.390 --> 00:13:32.010 align:middle line:84%
I agree that we
could make the error

00:13:32.010 --> 00:13:33.990 align:middle line:84%
rates in facial
recognition algorithms

00:13:33.990 --> 00:13:37.540 align:middle line:84%
equal for Whites and Blacks
and for men and women.

00:13:37.540 --> 00:13:40.470 align:middle line:84%
But what about all the other
racial and ethnic minorities?

00:13:40.470 --> 00:13:43.350 align:middle line:84%
And for that matter, what about
all the different genders?

00:13:43.350 --> 00:13:45.600 align:middle line:84%
Even if we could
agree on what groups

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:48.510 align:middle line:84%
need to have bias eliminated,
we can't practically

00:13:48.510 --> 00:13:49.680 align:middle line:90%
address them all.

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:52.290 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, and that's not
even how the field works.

00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:55.620 align:middle line:84%
No one's going to spend five
or 10 years for each algorithm

00:13:55.620 --> 00:13:57.180 align:middle line:90%
to eliminate bias.

00:13:57.180 --> 00:13:59.010 align:middle line:84%
For cutting-edge
research, people just

00:13:59.010 --> 00:14:03.300 align:middle line:84%
try to hit 80%, sometimes 90%
accuracy for a given algorithm

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:05.100 align:middle line:90%
and then publish a paper on it.

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:07.590 align:middle line:84%
The incentives are such
that they can't spend years

00:14:07.590 --> 00:14:11.520 align:middle line:84%
getting 99% or so accuracy
across the board, at least

00:14:11.520 --> 00:14:13.020 align:middle line:90%
for new algorithms.

00:14:13.020 --> 00:14:15.060 align:middle line:90%
There just isn't enough data.

00:14:15.060 --> 00:14:16.840 align:middle line:84%
Better to just get
something out there,

00:14:16.840 --> 00:14:20.460 align:middle line:84%
even if it's not so good, so
you at least get credit for it.

00:14:20.460 --> 00:14:22.500 align:middle line:84%
If we could only
eliminate randomness,

00:14:22.500 --> 00:14:24.390 align:middle line:90%
our problems would be solved.

00:14:24.390 --> 00:14:28.890 align:middle line:84%
But randomness is good because
it's the basis of choice,

00:14:28.890 --> 00:14:32.250 align:middle line:84%
otherwise our minds would just
follow a deterministic path

00:14:32.250 --> 00:14:35.190 align:middle line:84%
based on our neural pathways
and electrical signals.

00:14:35.190 --> 00:14:37.120 align:middle line:84%
The heart of it is
quantum mechanics,

00:14:37.120 --> 00:14:39.450 align:middle line:84%
which introduces
intrinsic randomness.

00:14:39.450 --> 00:14:43.230 align:middle line:84%
Well, randomness doesn't
give you choice, just chaos.

00:14:43.230 --> 00:14:44.910 align:middle line:84%
That's also the
reason we have bias

00:14:44.910 --> 00:14:48.660 align:middle line:84%
to begin with, because of
random historical circumstances.

00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:52.110 align:middle line:84%
We want to eliminate bias,
not just make it random.

00:14:52.110 --> 00:14:54.600 align:middle line:84%
If it is random, then
maybe you will mitigate it

00:14:54.600 --> 00:14:57.450 align:middle line:84%
for minorities, or
maybe, at least for some,

00:14:57.450 --> 00:14:59.100 align:middle line:90%
you'll find a way to augment it.

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:02.130 align:middle line:84%
[SCOFFS] That is what
I was saying, too.

00:15:02.130 --> 00:15:04.530 align:middle line:90%
So we seem to be stuck again.

00:15:04.530 --> 00:15:07.440 align:middle line:84%
Mathematics is the rigorous
way to understand the world,

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:09.390 align:middle line:90%
but it doesn't give us choice.

00:15:09.390 --> 00:15:11.250 align:middle line:84%
In the world described
by mathematics,

00:15:11.250 --> 00:15:13.710 align:middle line:90%
choice is an illusion.

00:15:13.710 --> 00:15:15.750 align:middle line:84%
Professor, we ran
into the same impasse

00:15:15.750 --> 00:15:19.020 align:middle line:84%
when we had this discussion
last week during spring break.

00:15:19.020 --> 00:15:21.460 align:middle line:90%
But how do we get out of it?

00:15:21.460 --> 00:15:23.770 align:middle line:84%
I think I can be
of some help here.

00:15:23.770 --> 00:15:28.200 align:middle line:84%
So you all agree that it is
desirable to eliminate bias,

00:15:28.200 --> 00:15:29.110 align:middle line:90%
right?

00:15:29.110 --> 00:15:29.610 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:15:29.610 --> 00:15:30.110 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:15:30.110 --> 00:15:33.120 align:middle line:84%
So you all think it is possible
to mitigate bias depending

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:34.600 align:middle line:90%
on what actions you take?

00:15:34.600 --> 00:15:35.100 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:15:35.100 --> 00:15:36.060 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:15:36.060 --> 00:15:40.110 align:middle line:84%
So you do think that can make
choices and not just randomly.

00:15:40.110 --> 00:15:42.060 align:middle line:84%
More well-grounded
choices are better than

00:15:42.060 --> 00:15:45.840 align:middle line:84%
less well-grounded ones,
say, for addressing bias.

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:48.990 align:middle line:84%
I don't think we really
have a choice about things.

00:15:48.990 --> 00:15:52.050 align:middle line:84%
People might think that they
do, but they don't really.

00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:55.830 align:middle line:84%
We can't choose where we are
born or how smart we are,

00:15:55.830 --> 00:15:58.980 align:middle line:84%
and we can't choose if
we get hit by a car.

00:15:58.980 --> 00:16:03.520 align:middle line:84%
We think we are making a
choice, but we really aren't.

00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:04.780 align:middle line:90%
It's like Sophie said.

00:16:04.780 --> 00:16:06.430 align:middle line:90%
It's all just an illusion.

00:16:06.430 --> 00:16:07.180 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:16:07.180 --> 00:16:08.260 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, you're right.

00:16:08.260 --> 00:16:10.060 align:middle line:90%
Randomness doesn't help.

00:16:10.060 --> 00:16:11.630 align:middle line:90%
You've convinced me.

00:16:11.630 --> 00:16:12.130 align:middle line:90%
Wait.

00:16:12.130 --> 00:16:15.080 align:middle line:84%
Did I have a choice, or
was I compelled to agree?

00:16:15.080 --> 00:16:15.580 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:16:15.580 --> 00:16:18.130 align:middle line:90%
Your neurons told you to agree.

00:16:18.130 --> 00:16:19.570 align:middle line:90%
Yes, exactly.

00:16:19.570 --> 00:16:24.490 align:middle line:84%
And randomness, a consequence
of a chaotic quantum process,

00:16:24.490 --> 00:16:26.800 align:middle line:84%
just makes what we
do not predictable

00:16:26.800 --> 00:16:29.620 align:middle line:84%
but doesn't mean that
we choose what we do.

00:16:29.620 --> 00:16:30.670 align:middle line:90%
Right.

00:16:30.670 --> 00:16:34.300 align:middle line:84%
Well, you have a choice whether
you're going to be biased

00:16:34.300 --> 00:16:35.230 align:middle line:90%
or not.

00:16:35.230 --> 00:16:35.830 align:middle line:90%
Mhm.

00:16:35.830 --> 00:16:37.040 align:middle line:90%
Indeed.

00:16:37.040 --> 00:16:39.580 align:middle line:84%
It does seem that
judgments involve choice.

00:16:39.580 --> 00:16:42.280 align:middle line:84%
But Alyssa, if you were
given the full understanding

00:16:42.280 --> 00:16:45.070 align:middle line:84%
of a situation, wouldn't you
always take the best action?

00:16:45.070 --> 00:16:46.510 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, of course.

00:16:46.510 --> 00:16:47.260 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:16:47.260 --> 00:16:50.530 align:middle line:84%
You can't really choose whether
anything is better or worse.

00:16:50.530 --> 00:16:52.540 align:middle line:90%
It's all just a value judgment.

00:16:52.540 --> 00:16:54.940 align:middle line:84%
You may be compelled to
do something by others,

00:16:54.940 --> 00:16:58.067 align:middle line:84%
but you never really
freely choose.

00:16:58.067 --> 00:16:59.650 align:middle line:84%
Do you really believe
that, or are you

00:16:59.650 --> 00:17:02.000 align:middle line:90%
just playing devil's advocate?

00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:02.500 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:17:02.500 --> 00:17:03.710 align:middle line:90%
A little of both.

00:17:03.710 --> 00:17:06.400 align:middle line:84%
But even if I am just
playing devil's advocate,

00:17:06.400 --> 00:17:07.960 align:middle line:84%
what's wrong with
what I'm saying?

00:17:07.960 --> 00:17:12.280 align:middle line:84%
What's wrong is that your model
of human life is inadequate.

00:17:12.280 --> 00:17:14.618 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't describe
what it really is.

00:17:14.618 --> 00:17:17.260 align:middle line:84%
We're not just some balls
moving around deterministically

00:17:17.260 --> 00:17:20.740 align:middle line:84%
with random noise interspersed
like a term in a Langevin

00:17:20.740 --> 00:17:22.329 align:middle line:90%
equation.

00:17:22.329 --> 00:17:24.579 align:middle line:84%
Isn't that true,
Professor Muller?

00:17:24.579 --> 00:17:26.550 align:middle line:90%
What a great discussion this is.

00:17:26.550 --> 00:17:28.300 align:middle line:84%
Sounds like you had
a much better spring

00:17:28.300 --> 00:17:31.180 align:middle line:84%
break discussing these questions
than going to the beach.

00:17:31.180 --> 00:17:32.410 align:middle line:90%
[CHUCKLES]

00:17:32.410 --> 00:17:34.370 align:middle line:84%
I think you're on the
right track, Sophie.

00:17:34.370 --> 00:17:34.870 align:middle line:90%
Funny.

00:17:34.870 --> 00:17:38.050 align:middle line:84%
Coincidentally, that's
my first name too.

00:17:38.050 --> 00:17:40.750 align:middle line:84%
At any rate, you have
unveiled a great tension

00:17:40.750 --> 00:17:42.580 align:middle line:90%
in modern science.

00:17:42.580 --> 00:17:45.520 align:middle line:84%
We are brought up to think
that modern science is the way

00:17:45.520 --> 00:17:47.890 align:middle line:84%
to describe the
world, but it seems

00:17:47.890 --> 00:17:50.230 align:middle line:84%
inadequate to
describe human things.

00:17:50.230 --> 00:17:53.290 align:middle line:84%
Ethical questions cannot be
reducible to mathematics--

00:17:53.290 --> 00:17:57.680 align:middle line:84%
similar to justice and
beauty, hope and suffering,

00:17:57.680 --> 00:18:00.970 align:middle line:84%
and, for that
matter, even truth.

00:18:00.970 --> 00:18:02.290 align:middle line:90%
Yes, Glenda.

00:18:02.290 --> 00:18:05.860 align:middle line:84%
Aren't those things even more
important than modern science?

00:18:05.860 --> 00:18:07.300 align:middle line:90%
I would say so.

00:18:07.300 --> 00:18:08.020 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:18:08.020 --> 00:18:11.080 align:middle line:84%
Those things are
arbitrary or a matter

00:18:11.080 --> 00:18:14.480 align:middle line:84%
of individual's interests--
what's in it for them.

00:18:14.480 --> 00:18:16.900 align:middle line:84%
That's the way to look
at them scientifically.

00:18:16.900 --> 00:18:20.490 align:middle line:84%
Bias exists because people
have conflicting interests.

00:18:20.490 --> 00:18:22.780 align:middle line:84%
Sure, if you look at it
purely scientifically,

00:18:22.780 --> 00:18:25.600 align:middle line:84%
but interest alone cannot
be adequate since people are

00:18:25.600 --> 00:18:28.720 align:middle line:84%
willing to sacrifice interests
for passions and, more

00:18:28.720 --> 00:18:31.690 align:middle line:84%
importantly, for things
beyond themselves.

00:18:31.690 --> 00:18:34.240 align:middle line:84%
At any rate, do most
of us really know

00:18:34.240 --> 00:18:35.800 align:middle line:90%
all of our interests?

00:18:35.800 --> 00:18:39.380 align:middle line:84%
We all have preferences that
go far beyond interests.

00:18:39.380 --> 00:18:43.390 align:middle line:84%
Perhaps we can say at best that
those interests are intertwined

00:18:43.390 --> 00:18:45.700 align:middle line:84%
with different
approaches to the world.

00:18:45.700 --> 00:18:49.090 align:middle line:84%
Besides, aren't the most
boring people the ones who say,

00:18:49.090 --> 00:18:52.660 align:middle line:84%
I'm right, and, even if one
can never make value judgments,

00:18:52.660 --> 00:18:54.910 align:middle line:84%
I have my own values
and I stick with them?

00:18:54.910 --> 00:18:58.630 align:middle line:84%
[CHUCKLES] Yeah, these are
the most irritating and boring

00:18:58.630 --> 00:18:59.920 align:middle line:90%
people.

00:18:59.920 --> 00:19:04.030 align:middle line:84%
So, now let's dig down a bit
more into the mathematics

00:19:04.030 --> 00:19:06.400 align:middle line:90%
of AI and the sorting problem.

00:19:06.400 --> 00:19:09.910 align:middle line:84%
Perhaps it's best to start
with a specific example.

00:19:09.910 --> 00:19:13.270 align:middle line:84%
What's the reason for the
bias in facial recognition

00:19:13.270 --> 00:19:15.040 align:middle line:84%
that we discussed
a little while ago?

00:19:15.040 --> 00:19:18.210 align:middle line:84%
A biased training set due
to historical inequity.

00:19:18.210 --> 00:19:18.970 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:19:18.970 --> 00:19:21.280 align:middle line:84%
So one of the issues
is the training set.

00:19:21.280 --> 00:19:23.200 align:middle line:84%
The other issue
is the algorithm,

00:19:23.200 --> 00:19:25.540 align:middle line:84%
including its hyperparameters,
all leading to how

00:19:25.540 --> 00:19:27.400 align:middle line:90%
the classification is done.

00:19:27.400 --> 00:19:30.070 align:middle line:84%
This is why different algorithms
give different error rates

00:19:30.070 --> 00:19:32.050 align:middle line:90%
using the same training data.

00:19:32.050 --> 00:19:33.010 align:middle line:90%
Anything else?

00:19:33.010 --> 00:19:36.270 align:middle line:90%


00:19:36.270 --> 00:19:36.960 align:middle line:90%
Right.

00:19:36.960 --> 00:19:39.670 align:middle line:90%
So those are the two key issues.

00:19:39.670 --> 00:19:42.000 align:middle line:84%
However, within the
choice of algorithm,

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:44.530 align:middle line:84%
there is the problem
with classification.

00:19:44.530 --> 00:19:46.680 align:middle line:84%
Let me project
this data set, here

00:19:46.680 --> 00:19:48.450 align:middle line:90%
with points in red and blue.

00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:52.030 align:middle line:84%
You have many choices for
putting the dividing surface.

00:19:52.030 --> 00:19:54.510 align:middle line:84%
But what if we
choose the wrong one?

00:19:54.510 --> 00:19:57.510 align:middle line:84%
Some of the data may be
misclassified, won't it?

00:19:57.510 --> 00:19:58.500 align:middle line:90%
You mean like this?

00:19:58.500 --> 00:20:00.180 align:middle line:90%
Yes, exactly.

00:20:00.180 --> 00:20:04.390 align:middle line:84%
So how do you choose the
right line ahead of time?

00:20:04.390 --> 00:20:06.420 align:middle line:90%
That is a problem.

00:20:06.420 --> 00:20:10.310 align:middle line:84%
It could be even worse if
your data set looks like this.

00:20:10.310 --> 00:20:12.970 align:middle line:84%
But then we don't have
to stick with lines.

00:20:12.970 --> 00:20:14.620 align:middle line:84%
Why don't you draw
a curve around them?

00:20:14.620 --> 00:20:15.370 align:middle line:90%
Like this?

00:20:15.370 --> 00:20:17.200 align:middle line:90%
Yes, exactly.

00:20:17.200 --> 00:20:19.210 align:middle line:84%
Now you've classified
them all perfectly.

00:20:19.210 --> 00:20:20.173 align:middle line:90%
Well, wait a second.

00:20:20.173 --> 00:20:22.090 align:middle line:84%
If you do that, it works
for the training set,

00:20:22.090 --> 00:20:24.580 align:middle line:84%
but it may not work for
any additional data sets.

00:20:24.580 --> 00:20:27.010 align:middle line:84%
In fact, it might be
even worse than the lines

00:20:27.010 --> 00:20:29.350 align:middle line:84%
because you're overfitting
to the training data.

00:20:29.350 --> 00:20:31.090 align:middle line:90%
Do you mean like this?

00:20:31.090 --> 00:20:32.050 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:20:32.050 --> 00:20:33.280 align:middle line:90%
That doesn't work.

00:20:33.280 --> 00:20:34.840 align:middle line:90%
Oh, yes.

00:20:34.840 --> 00:20:35.740 align:middle line:90%
I see it.

00:20:35.740 --> 00:20:36.790 align:middle line:90%
Exactly.

00:20:36.790 --> 00:20:39.250 align:middle line:84%
Remember this is a sorting
algorithm, here in two

00:20:39.250 --> 00:20:42.925 align:middle line:84%
dimensions with two descriptors,
thus it is quite limited.

00:20:42.925 --> 00:20:45.550 align:middle line:84%
But even if you do it in a very
large number of dimensions with

00:20:45.550 --> 00:20:47.470 align:middle line:84%
a very large number
of descriptors,

00:20:47.470 --> 00:20:49.810 align:middle line:84%
you are still projecting
a complicated system--

00:20:49.810 --> 00:20:51.910 align:middle line:90%
shall we say a natural system--

00:20:51.910 --> 00:20:54.610 align:middle line:84%
onto an artificial
mathematical construct

00:20:54.610 --> 00:20:58.030 align:middle line:84%
that necessarily leaves
much out of the description.

00:20:58.030 --> 00:21:01.390 align:middle line:84%
Or if you want to stay in
the realm of mathematics,

00:21:01.390 --> 00:21:04.150 align:middle line:84%
you could say that due to
a necessary incomplete set

00:21:04.150 --> 00:21:07.990 align:middle line:84%
of descriptors you
were fitting noise.

00:21:07.990 --> 00:21:11.230 align:middle line:84%
This allows you to get a perfect
fit for your training set,

00:21:11.230 --> 00:21:13.570 align:middle line:84%
but then you have an even
worse fit for your data set

00:21:13.570 --> 00:21:15.780 align:middle line:90%
moving forward.

00:21:15.780 --> 00:21:17.400 align:middle line:90%
Patch.

00:21:17.400 --> 00:21:19.890 align:middle line:84%
In other words, we
have to accept the fact

00:21:19.890 --> 00:21:21.790 align:middle line:90%
that there will be errors.

00:21:21.790 --> 00:21:24.960 align:middle line:84%
But at least we could distribute
the errors evenly among groups.

00:21:24.960 --> 00:21:27.340 align:middle line:90%
That is what Alyssa said before.

00:21:27.340 --> 00:21:29.940 align:middle line:84%
We can try, but
then I'm worried we

00:21:29.940 --> 00:21:32.110 align:middle line:90%
won't include all the groups.

00:21:32.110 --> 00:21:35.460 align:middle line:84%
Even if we could, there'll
be errors in the errors.

00:21:35.460 --> 00:21:37.590 align:middle line:84%
Unless we have a very,
very large training

00:21:37.590 --> 00:21:40.500 align:middle line:84%
set, which practically
we never have,

00:21:40.500 --> 00:21:42.120 align:middle line:90%
we won't be able to do that.

00:21:42.120 --> 00:21:42.960 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:21:42.960 --> 00:21:44.790 align:middle line:84%
The training set would
have to incorporate

00:21:44.790 --> 00:21:46.350 align:middle line:90%
all the data in the world.

00:21:46.350 --> 00:21:48.390 align:middle line:84%
You know, that
reminds me of a story

00:21:48.390 --> 00:21:51.030 align:middle line:84%
I once heard, I think from
a South American writer,

00:21:51.030 --> 00:21:53.820 align:middle line:84%
where someone makes a one-to-one
scale map of their country

00:21:53.820 --> 00:21:55.740 align:middle line:90%
with all the details included.

00:21:55.740 --> 00:21:56.550 align:middle line:90%
[CHUCKLES]

00:21:56.550 --> 00:21:58.980 align:middle line:90%
Ha, ha.

00:21:58.980 --> 00:22:00.570 align:middle line:90%
Well, I grant the problem.

00:22:00.570 --> 00:22:03.550 align:middle line:90%
But then what do we do?

00:22:03.550 --> 00:22:06.570 align:middle line:84%
We all agree that we
need to eliminate bias,

00:22:06.570 --> 00:22:10.800 align:middle line:90%
but it seems so hard.

00:22:10.800 --> 00:22:15.060 align:middle line:84%
What if we could at least
have a very extensive model

00:22:15.060 --> 00:22:20.260 align:middle line:84%
that for all practical purposes
captures a given situation?

00:22:20.260 --> 00:22:23.700 align:middle line:84%
Which I guess is
actually impossible

00:22:23.700 --> 00:22:26.700 align:middle line:84%
because it would require a
complete and accurate model

00:22:26.700 --> 00:22:29.100 align:middle line:84%
of all the objects
in the whole world.

00:22:29.100 --> 00:22:29.800 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:22:29.800 --> 00:22:32.550 align:middle line:84%
As engineers, we can at
least work to do that.

00:22:32.550 --> 00:22:36.750 align:middle line:84%
If the model is extensive
enough, we can eliminate bias.

00:22:36.750 --> 00:22:40.470 align:middle line:84%
But then we'd be eliminating any
choice that we might have had.

00:22:40.470 --> 00:22:43.650 align:middle line:84%
We would be controlled
by an algorithm.

00:22:43.650 --> 00:22:49.200 align:middle line:84%
That sounds-- that
sounds tyrannical.

00:22:49.200 --> 00:22:49.980 align:middle line:90%
Indeed.

00:22:49.980 --> 00:22:53.220 align:middle line:84%
Mathematization of human
things distorts them so much

00:22:53.220 --> 00:22:55.680 align:middle line:84%
that they become something
which they are not.

00:22:55.680 --> 00:22:58.500 align:middle line:84%
This is actually the
utilitarian approach.

00:22:58.500 --> 00:23:03.690 align:middle line:84%
Human things like joy, anger,
justice, honor, beauty,

00:23:03.690 --> 00:23:07.170 align:middle line:84%
and happiness are really
not mathematizable.

00:23:07.170 --> 00:23:09.150 align:middle line:84%
We lose all of what
is important in them

00:23:09.150 --> 00:23:11.160 align:middle line:90%
when we mathematize them.

00:23:11.160 --> 00:23:15.390 align:middle line:84%
We could in principle try to
do so and therein transform

00:23:15.390 --> 00:23:16.155 align:middle line:90%
human reality.

00:23:16.155 --> 00:23:19.260 align:middle line:90%


00:23:19.260 --> 00:23:20.370 align:middle line:90%
Glenda.

00:23:20.370 --> 00:23:22.680 align:middle line:90%
That doesn't sound appealing.

00:23:22.680 --> 00:23:24.990 align:middle line:90%
It sounds dreadful.

00:23:24.990 --> 00:23:27.090 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that wouldn't be good.

00:23:27.090 --> 00:23:30.570 align:middle line:84%
But we can at least do
it partially, can't we?

00:23:30.570 --> 00:23:31.260 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:23:31.260 --> 00:23:33.060 align:middle line:84%
Maybe that would
be a good solution.

00:23:33.060 --> 00:23:33.960 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:23:33.960 --> 00:23:36.120 align:middle line:84%
Even if those human
things cannot be described

00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:39.600 align:middle line:84%
by mathematics, it is still the
only rigorous way to understand

00:23:39.600 --> 00:23:40.320 align:middle line:90%
the world.

00:23:40.320 --> 00:23:45.300 align:middle line:84%
I'm not so sure anymore, but
the bias is in the data set,

00:23:45.300 --> 00:23:47.310 align:middle line:90%
and it is in the algorithms.

00:23:47.310 --> 00:23:52.050 align:middle line:84%
So we must be able to adjust
our models to solve the problem.

00:23:52.050 --> 00:23:53.370 align:middle line:90%
Mustn't we?

00:23:53.370 --> 00:23:56.700 align:middle line:84%
Well, consider this-- our
recognition of the problem

00:23:56.700 --> 00:23:58.860 align:middle line:90%
of bias is not mathematical.

00:23:58.860 --> 00:24:02.400 align:middle line:84%
Mathematics comes only
after such recognition.

00:24:02.400 --> 00:24:04.800 align:middle line:84%
Mathematics is something
we impose on our broader

00:24:04.800 --> 00:24:06.550 align:middle line:90%
understanding of the world.

00:24:06.550 --> 00:24:09.300 align:middle line:84%
We can use it to develop
models, but they will always

00:24:09.300 --> 00:24:12.120 align:middle line:84%
be just that, models
which are necessarily

00:24:12.120 --> 00:24:14.280 align:middle line:90%
incomplete descriptions.

00:24:14.280 --> 00:24:20.400 align:middle line:84%
I see what you're
saying, I think.

00:24:20.400 --> 00:24:21.390 align:middle line:90%
Sophie.

00:24:21.390 --> 00:24:24.310 align:middle line:90%
But AI is based on mathematics.

00:24:24.310 --> 00:24:27.600 align:middle line:84%
So we have to fix this
problem in the algorithms

00:24:27.600 --> 00:24:30.240 align:middle line:90%
via fixing the algorithms, i.e.

00:24:30.240 --> 00:24:31.680 align:middle line:90%
by using mathematics.

00:24:31.680 --> 00:24:32.880 align:middle line:90%
Don't we?

00:24:32.880 --> 00:24:35.160 align:middle line:84%
Well, we can think
of things this way,

00:24:35.160 --> 00:24:37.950 align:middle line:84%
but that will lead us
to a double whammy.

00:24:37.950 --> 00:24:40.170 align:middle line:84%
We cannot develop a
non-arbitrary definition

00:24:40.170 --> 00:24:43.800 align:middle line:84%
of the criteria for the
training set, and, two,

00:24:43.800 --> 00:24:46.290 align:middle line:84%
we cannot develop a
non-arbitrary definition

00:24:46.290 --> 00:24:50.850 align:middle line:84%
of the error target for
the sorting algorithm.

00:24:50.850 --> 00:24:53.340 align:middle line:84%
Well, I knew that we
were at an impasse,

00:24:53.340 --> 00:24:56.070 align:middle line:84%
but I didn't think it
was this big of one.

00:24:56.070 --> 00:24:57.540 align:middle line:84%
Professor, your
class has made us

00:24:57.540 --> 00:24:59.920 align:middle line:90%
more stuck than we were before.

00:24:59.920 --> 00:25:02.640 align:middle line:90%
So, what do we do?

00:25:02.640 --> 00:25:04.740 align:middle line:90%
Is there another way?

00:25:04.740 --> 00:25:06.400 align:middle line:90%
Indeed, Glenda, there is that.

00:25:06.400 --> 00:25:10.633 align:middle line:84%
It requires a reconsideration of
our concerns and of the issue.

00:25:10.633 --> 00:25:14.084 align:middle line:90%
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:25:14.084 --> 00:25:25.916 align:middle line:90%


00:25:25.916 --> 00:25:27.395 align:middle line:90%
[DOOR CLOSES]

00:25:27.395 --> 00:25:40.230 align:middle line:90%


00:25:40.230 --> 00:25:40.760 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

00:25:40.760 --> 00:25:44.550 align:middle line:90%


00:25:44.550 --> 00:25:45.720 align:middle line:90%
Sophie.

00:25:45.720 --> 00:25:48.280 align:middle line:90%
So, what is the other way?

00:25:48.280 --> 00:25:49.710 align:middle line:90%
Well, let's think about it.

00:25:49.710 --> 00:25:52.650 align:middle line:84%
We just showed that because of
the intrinsic incompleteness

00:25:52.650 --> 00:25:55.170 align:middle line:84%
of models eliminating
bias in all dimensions

00:25:55.170 --> 00:25:57.210 align:middle line:90%
is mathematically impossible.

00:25:57.210 --> 00:26:00.120 align:middle line:84%
We could no doubt decrease
bias among some groups

00:26:00.120 --> 00:26:03.540 align:middle line:84%
to a certain degree, with an
acceptably small but still

00:26:03.540 --> 00:26:05.610 align:middle line:90%
significant margin of error.

00:26:05.610 --> 00:26:08.520 align:middle line:84%
Even if we got rid of bias in
some predefined dimensions,

00:26:08.520 --> 00:26:10.800 align:middle line:84%
we would still have it
in other dimensions.

00:26:10.800 --> 00:26:12.900 align:middle line:90%
But that's precisely it.

00:26:12.900 --> 00:26:15.840 align:middle line:84%
We want equality in at
least some dimensions,

00:26:15.840 --> 00:26:17.040 align:middle line:90%
the important ones.

00:26:17.040 --> 00:26:17.910 align:middle line:90%
That is good.

00:26:17.910 --> 00:26:20.760 align:middle line:84%
How do you choose which
dimensions are important?

00:26:20.760 --> 00:26:21.420 align:middle line:90%
Alvis.

00:26:21.420 --> 00:26:23.730 align:middle line:84%
One important
dimension is wealth.

00:26:23.730 --> 00:26:25.350 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:26:25.350 --> 00:26:28.500 align:middle line:84%
So even you five, the
top engineering students

00:26:28.500 --> 00:26:31.320 align:middle line:84%
in your class at the top
engineering school, should get

00:26:31.320 --> 00:26:35.130 align:middle line:84%
paid just as much as someone
like Dean, the paper collector.

00:26:35.130 --> 00:26:40.350 align:middle line:84%
Well, he is a very nice
fellow, if a bit strange.

00:26:40.350 --> 00:26:44.550 align:middle line:84%
But no, we think we should be
rewarded for the contributions

00:26:44.550 --> 00:26:46.530 align:middle line:90%
that we make to society.

00:26:46.530 --> 00:26:49.648 align:middle line:84%
But wealth should be
distributed more equally.

00:26:49.648 --> 00:26:50.190 align:middle line:90%
That's right.

00:26:50.190 --> 00:26:52.110 align:middle line:84%
But how do you decide
how equal it should be?

00:26:52.110 --> 00:26:55.290 align:middle line:90%


00:26:55.290 --> 00:26:57.240 align:middle line:84%
Perhaps the problem
is once again

00:26:57.240 --> 00:27:00.360 align:middle line:84%
that mathematics in its
homogenization of kinds

00:27:00.360 --> 00:27:03.090 align:middle line:84%
is not the right way
to address the problem.

00:27:03.090 --> 00:27:04.650 align:middle line:90%
Let me ask you this--

00:27:04.650 --> 00:27:07.920 align:middle line:84%
is it really equality that
you want, or is it justice?

00:27:07.920 --> 00:27:08.760 align:middle line:90%
Justice.

00:27:08.760 --> 00:27:09.450 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:27:09.450 --> 00:27:11.250 align:middle line:90%
I'll go with justice.

00:27:11.250 --> 00:27:14.580 align:middle line:84%
Great that you two
agree on something.

00:27:14.580 --> 00:27:15.720 align:middle line:90%
Glenda.

00:27:15.720 --> 00:27:18.600 align:middle line:84%
They should go hand in
hand, justice and equality.

00:27:18.600 --> 00:27:19.140 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:27:19.140 --> 00:27:20.970 align:middle line:90%
Isn't equality justice?

00:27:20.970 --> 00:27:23.280 align:middle line:84%
I think we need to add
another consideration,

00:27:23.280 --> 00:27:25.560 align:middle line:84%
since we are beginning to
delve into the complex human

00:27:25.560 --> 00:27:27.600 align:middle line:84%
questions underlying
our definition

00:27:27.600 --> 00:27:29.680 align:middle line:90%
and perception of bias.

00:27:29.680 --> 00:27:30.850 align:middle line:90%
So let me ask you this.

00:27:30.850 --> 00:27:33.780 align:middle line:84%
Which is most important--
equality, justice,

00:27:33.780 --> 00:27:36.370 align:middle line:90%
or happiness?

00:27:36.370 --> 00:27:37.540 align:middle line:90%
Sophie.

00:27:37.540 --> 00:27:39.700 align:middle line:90%
I would say happiness.

00:27:39.700 --> 00:27:41.450 align:middle line:84%
Even if someone has
more than you do,

00:27:41.450 --> 00:27:43.360 align:middle line:84%
and even if they
don't deserve it,

00:27:43.360 --> 00:27:46.540 align:middle line:84%
it doesn't matter to you
as long as you are happy.

00:27:46.540 --> 00:27:49.270 align:middle line:84%
Think of all the people
born into rich families.

00:27:49.270 --> 00:27:51.100 align:middle line:84%
They're typically
not happy since they

00:27:51.100 --> 00:27:54.310 align:middle line:84%
don't need to have a goal, and
therefore they don't have one.

00:27:54.310 --> 00:27:57.213 align:middle line:84%
I would still have a goal if I
were born into a rich family.

00:27:57.213 --> 00:27:58.630 align:middle line:84%
Why do they need
a goal if they're

00:27:58.630 --> 00:28:00.250 align:middle line:90%
having such a good time?

00:28:00.250 --> 00:28:03.400 align:middle line:84%
Because people need to
have a purpose to be happy.

00:28:03.400 --> 00:28:07.270 align:middle line:84%
And how can people be happy
if they're not treated fairly?

00:28:07.270 --> 00:28:10.550 align:middle line:84%
So we have equality,
justice, and happiness,

00:28:10.550 --> 00:28:13.674 align:middle line:84%
but it is unclear which
is most important.

00:28:13.674 --> 00:28:14.620 align:middle line:90%
Glenda.

00:28:14.620 --> 00:28:16.600 align:middle line:84%
Can't they be of
equal importance?

00:28:16.600 --> 00:28:17.830 align:middle line:90%
Actually, no.

00:28:17.830 --> 00:28:19.510 align:middle line:90%
They are certainly interrelated.

00:28:19.510 --> 00:28:22.580 align:middle line:84%
But since they are at
least somewhat different

00:28:22.580 --> 00:28:25.030 align:middle line:84%
and since, as we saw, they
can conflict, one of them

00:28:25.030 --> 00:28:28.670 align:middle line:84%
has to be most important, has to
take precedence over the other.

00:28:28.670 --> 00:28:31.600 align:middle line:84%
I think that justice is more
important than equality.

00:28:31.600 --> 00:28:34.870 align:middle line:84%
After all, we don't want
everyone to be the exact same,

00:28:34.870 --> 00:28:38.980 align:middle line:84%
but still justice and
equality are related.

00:28:38.980 --> 00:28:42.220 align:middle line:84%
People can be unequal because
they're treated unfairly.

00:28:42.220 --> 00:28:45.340 align:middle line:84%
They can also be unequal
due to luck, good or bad.

00:28:45.340 --> 00:28:47.770 align:middle line:84%
I don't think that happiness
can be the standard.

00:28:47.770 --> 00:28:50.860 align:middle line:84%
We need equality, and that's
why mathematics is still

00:28:50.860 --> 00:28:53.800 align:middle line:84%
so important, because we
can employ it rigorously

00:28:53.800 --> 00:28:55.510 align:middle line:90%
to make everyone equal.

00:28:55.510 --> 00:28:59.350 align:middle line:84%
But if everyone is the same,
wouldn't the world be drab?

00:28:59.350 --> 00:29:01.360 align:middle line:84%
I mean, what if all
of us were the same?

00:29:01.360 --> 00:29:04.360 align:middle line:84%
We wouldn't even be able to
have this dialogue, as we'd all

00:29:04.360 --> 00:29:05.992 align:middle line:90%
be making the same point.

00:29:05.992 --> 00:29:07.450 align:middle line:84%
There would be
nothing interesting,

00:29:07.450 --> 00:29:11.290 align:middle line:84%
as everyone is reduced
to a forced homogeneity.

00:29:11.290 --> 00:29:13.120 align:middle line:84%
Besides, how do
you make everyone

00:29:13.120 --> 00:29:15.340 align:middle line:84%
equal without
violating their rights?

00:29:15.340 --> 00:29:17.710 align:middle line:84%
We're going to have to
violate some rights to make

00:29:17.710 --> 00:29:19.460 align:middle line:90%
people at least more equal.

00:29:19.460 --> 00:29:23.290 align:middle line:84%
But then maybe I guess
that's not justice.

00:29:23.290 --> 00:29:23.920 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:29:23.920 --> 00:29:27.130 align:middle line:84%
Isn't it the essence of tyranny
when rights are violated

00:29:27.130 --> 00:29:28.810 align:middle line:90%
in the name of justice?

00:29:28.810 --> 00:29:29.680 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:29:29.680 --> 00:29:32.050 align:middle line:84%
We have strong reasons
not to want that.

00:29:32.050 --> 00:29:34.030 align:middle line:84%
We want equality,
but we certainly

00:29:34.030 --> 00:29:37.290 align:middle line:84%
don't want to violate
people's rights to get it.

00:29:37.290 --> 00:29:39.710 align:middle line:90%
But what do we do?

00:29:39.710 --> 00:29:41.750 align:middle line:90%
Here we are at an impasse again.

00:29:41.750 --> 00:29:43.400 align:middle line:84%
How do we understand
this conflict

00:29:43.400 --> 00:29:46.430 align:middle line:90%
between equality and rights?

00:29:46.430 --> 00:29:48.650 align:middle line:84%
By the way, professor,
what about that other way

00:29:48.650 --> 00:29:49.670 align:middle line:90%
that you promised?

00:29:49.670 --> 00:29:52.960 align:middle line:90%
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:29:52.960 --> 00:29:54.840 align:middle line:90%


00:29:54.840 --> 00:29:57.455 align:middle line:84%
I was wondering when you
would want to go to that.

00:29:57.455 --> 00:29:59.580 align:middle line:84%
Do you remember learning
about modern natural right

00:29:59.580 --> 00:30:02.140 align:middle line:84%
from Professor Baer
before spring break?

00:30:02.140 --> 00:30:03.430 align:middle line:90%
Yes, a bit.

00:30:03.430 --> 00:30:05.970 align:middle line:84%
We studied Locke's state of
nature and its connection

00:30:05.970 --> 00:30:09.120 align:middle line:84%
with natural right, if that's
what you're talking about.

00:30:09.120 --> 00:30:10.740 align:middle line:84%
Professor Baer
also made the case

00:30:10.740 --> 00:30:14.290 align:middle line:84%
that modern natural right is the
basis for the American regime

00:30:14.290 --> 00:30:16.510 align:middle line:84%
and the reason for
this country's success.

00:30:16.510 --> 00:30:17.010 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:30:17.010 --> 00:30:18.900 align:middle line:84%
And yes, that is
exactly what I mean.

00:30:18.900 --> 00:30:20.400 align:middle line:84%
It's great that you
remember reading

00:30:20.400 --> 00:30:22.020 align:middle line:84%
Locke and about
modern natural right

00:30:22.020 --> 00:30:25.730 align:middle line:84%
being the basis for
the American regime.

00:30:25.730 --> 00:30:26.230 align:middle line:90%
Oh.

00:30:26.230 --> 00:30:27.630 align:middle line:90%
Hello, Professor Knowles.

00:30:27.630 --> 00:30:28.890 align:middle line:90%
What are you doing here?

00:30:28.890 --> 00:30:30.990 align:middle line:90%
I hope I'm not bothering you.

00:30:30.990 --> 00:30:33.540 align:middle line:84%
I heard that you were
discussing modern natural right,

00:30:33.540 --> 00:30:36.420 align:middle line:90%
and I wanted to join.

00:30:36.420 --> 00:30:38.340 align:middle line:84%
The stuff you heard
me spew earlier

00:30:38.340 --> 00:30:41.040 align:middle line:84%
was a front that I
have to put forward

00:30:41.040 --> 00:30:43.470 align:middle line:84%
to my colleagues in the
philosophy department,

00:30:43.470 --> 00:30:46.770 align:middle line:84%
and some students who
appreciate such things.

00:30:46.770 --> 00:30:49.200 align:middle line:84%
It's the only way I
could keep a job, which

00:30:49.200 --> 00:30:52.245 align:middle line:84%
I need to be able to
pursue true philosophy.

00:30:52.245 --> 00:30:53.370 align:middle line:90%
That is refreshing to hear.

00:30:53.370 --> 00:30:54.900 align:middle line:90%
And yes, please do join us.

00:30:54.900 --> 00:30:58.110 align:middle line:84%
We were just starting
on modern natural right.

00:30:58.110 --> 00:31:03.690 align:middle line:84%
So how does Locke view the
problem of AI and bias?

00:31:03.690 --> 00:31:04.590 align:middle line:90%
Patch.

00:31:04.590 --> 00:31:07.530 align:middle line:84%
Locke would think it
wrong because bias

00:31:07.530 --> 00:31:09.600 align:middle line:90%
violates your natural right.

00:31:09.600 --> 00:31:11.400 align:middle line:90%
Yes, I agree.

00:31:11.400 --> 00:31:15.300 align:middle line:84%
That sounds right,
but I'm not sure

00:31:15.300 --> 00:31:19.620 align:middle line:84%
because we said that justice is
more important than equality.

00:31:19.620 --> 00:31:24.180 align:middle line:84%
But it seems that equality
is fundamental in Locke.

00:31:24.180 --> 00:31:27.300 align:middle line:84%
But then where is
justice in Locke?

00:31:27.300 --> 00:31:29.880 align:middle line:90%
Let's take this step by step.

00:31:29.880 --> 00:31:31.500 align:middle line:84%
Does anyone remember
the beginning?

00:31:31.500 --> 00:31:34.210 align:middle line:90%


00:31:34.210 --> 00:31:36.210 align:middle line:84%
But you said you were
working on these questions

00:31:36.210 --> 00:31:38.160 align:middle line:90%
during spring break.

00:31:38.160 --> 00:31:38.760 align:middle line:90%
Alvis?

00:31:38.760 --> 00:31:42.870 align:middle line:84%
Well, we were discussing them,
but we never actually went back

00:31:42.870 --> 00:31:45.030 align:middle line:90%
to the texts from class.

00:31:45.030 --> 00:31:45.600 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:31:45.600 --> 00:31:49.300 align:middle line:84%
We just wanted to figure
them out for ourselves.

00:31:49.300 --> 00:31:52.560 align:middle line:84%
I guess you disapprove,
Professor Muller?

00:31:52.560 --> 00:31:54.030 align:middle line:90%
On the contrary.

00:31:54.030 --> 00:31:55.800 align:middle line:84%
You are a very
impressive group to have

00:31:55.800 --> 00:31:57.870 align:middle line:84%
stayed here over spring
break and investigated

00:31:57.870 --> 00:31:58.800 align:middle line:90%
these questions.

00:31:58.800 --> 00:32:02.100 align:middle line:84%
Any teacher would be thrilled to
have even one student like you,

00:32:02.100 --> 00:32:03.810 align:middle line:90%
and I have five.

00:32:03.810 --> 00:32:06.000 align:middle line:90%
But you said that you got stuck.

00:32:06.000 --> 00:32:08.010 align:middle line:84%
What was your plan to
get out of your impasse?

00:32:08.010 --> 00:32:09.840 align:middle line:90%
Well, your class, of course.

00:32:09.840 --> 00:32:11.070 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHS]

00:32:11.070 --> 00:32:12.150 align:middle line:90%
Hush, Patch.

00:32:12.150 --> 00:32:15.000 align:middle line:84%
You seem not to realize
that Professor Muller is

00:32:15.000 --> 00:32:17.520 align:middle line:84%
in a most mild way
rebuking us for not

00:32:17.520 --> 00:32:19.680 align:middle line:90%
using the resources at hand.

00:32:19.680 --> 00:32:21.900 align:middle line:90%
Well, let's use them now.

00:32:21.900 --> 00:32:24.660 align:middle line:84%
Professor, please remind us
of Locke's starting point.

00:32:24.660 --> 00:32:25.630 align:middle line:90%
Of course.

00:32:25.630 --> 00:32:28.170 align:middle line:84%
Let's turn to second
treatise, chapter 1.

00:32:28.170 --> 00:32:31.020 align:middle line:90%


00:32:31.020 --> 00:32:33.850 align:middle line:84%
Professor Knowles, where do
you think we should start?

00:32:33.850 --> 00:32:36.660 align:middle line:84%
I'd say the last section
of paragraph one,

00:32:36.660 --> 00:32:38.820 align:middle line:90%
starting with "he that."

00:32:38.820 --> 00:32:40.680 align:middle line:84%
That is exactly
what I was thinking.

00:32:40.680 --> 00:32:42.480 align:middle line:84%
Glenda, could you
read the statement

00:32:42.480 --> 00:32:44.550 align:middle line:90%
starting with "he that?"

00:32:44.550 --> 00:32:45.790 align:middle line:90%
Of course.

00:32:45.790 --> 00:32:47.820 align:middle line:84%
He that will not
give just occasion

00:32:47.820 --> 00:32:49.560 align:middle line:84%
to think that all
government in the world

00:32:49.560 --> 00:32:51.630 align:middle line:84%
is the product of only
force and violence

00:32:51.630 --> 00:32:54.510 align:middle line:84%
must of necessity find out
another rise of government.

00:32:54.510 --> 00:32:55.890 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:32:55.890 --> 00:32:58.320 align:middle line:84%
So Locke is giving
us two options

00:32:58.320 --> 00:33:02.250 align:middle line:84%
of the origin of government,
force or something else--

00:33:02.250 --> 00:33:04.290 align:middle line:84%
maybe something
else based on reason

00:33:04.290 --> 00:33:07.110 align:middle line:84%
but not narrow or
mathematical reason.

00:33:07.110 --> 00:33:08.310 align:middle line:90%
Yes, of course.

00:33:08.310 --> 00:33:09.900 align:middle line:90%
That is much better.

00:33:09.900 --> 00:33:12.360 align:middle line:84%
It would be terrible to
think that politics is just

00:33:12.360 --> 00:33:13.320 align:middle line:90%
about force.

00:33:13.320 --> 00:33:14.250 align:middle line:90%
I agree.

00:33:14.250 --> 00:33:15.690 align:middle line:84%
It should be about
allowing people

00:33:15.690 --> 00:33:18.390 align:middle line:90%
to be happy, as we said before.

00:33:18.390 --> 00:33:22.400 align:middle line:84%
But what does this
alternative basis?

00:33:22.400 --> 00:33:25.170 align:middle line:84%
You can look at your
books, you know.

00:33:25.170 --> 00:33:25.670 align:middle line:90%
Alvis.

00:33:25.670 --> 00:33:27.000 align:middle line:90%
It is the state of nature.

00:33:27.000 --> 00:33:27.500 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:33:27.500 --> 00:33:29.360 align:middle line:90%
What is the state of nature?

00:33:29.360 --> 00:33:30.260 align:middle line:90%
Well, let's see.

00:33:30.260 --> 00:33:33.560 align:middle line:84%
I actually underlined
this pretty extensively.

00:33:33.560 --> 00:33:37.910 align:middle line:84%
It is a state of perfect freedom
and a state also of equality.

00:33:37.910 --> 00:33:38.660 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:33:38.660 --> 00:33:40.130 align:middle line:90%
I remember now.

00:33:40.130 --> 00:33:43.730 align:middle line:84%
But how can it be a
state of perfect freedom

00:33:43.730 --> 00:33:47.540 align:middle line:84%
if anyone can take your
stuff and it's only up to you

00:33:47.540 --> 00:33:49.430 align:middle line:90%
to protect it?

00:33:49.430 --> 00:33:50.700 align:middle line:90%
You don't remember?

00:33:50.700 --> 00:33:51.800 align:middle line:90%
That's the point.

00:33:51.800 --> 00:33:53.570 align:middle line:84%
There is no government,
so we're all

00:33:53.570 --> 00:33:55.430 align:middle line:90%
perfectly free in that sense.

00:33:55.430 --> 00:33:57.980 align:middle line:84%
But life is not
desirable since we're

00:33:57.980 --> 00:34:00.260 align:middle line:90%
all individually vulnerable.

00:34:00.260 --> 00:34:03.020 align:middle line:84%
That's why we form
governments, for security.

00:34:03.020 --> 00:34:05.700 align:middle line:84%
Very good, Sophie, but
you're moving too fast.

00:34:05.700 --> 00:34:09.050 align:middle line:84%
Remember Locke says but though
this be a state of liberty,

00:34:09.050 --> 00:34:11.420 align:middle line:84%
yet it is not a
state of license.

00:34:11.420 --> 00:34:13.040 align:middle line:90%
Do you recall why that is?

00:34:13.040 --> 00:34:14.090 align:middle line:90%
Oh, hold on.

00:34:14.090 --> 00:34:15.230 align:middle line:90%
I have it right here.

00:34:15.230 --> 00:34:18.590 align:middle line:90%
Because of a law of nature.

00:34:18.590 --> 00:34:19.429 align:middle line:90%
Yes, that's it.

00:34:19.429 --> 00:34:22.010 align:middle line:84%
Laws of nature are
based on reason.

00:34:22.010 --> 00:34:22.639 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:34:22.639 --> 00:34:24.500 align:middle line:84%
We talked about that
before spring break.

00:34:24.500 --> 00:34:25.699 align:middle line:90%
Now I remember.

00:34:25.699 --> 00:34:29.333 align:middle line:84%
It's dumb to waste stuff,
including your own life.

00:34:29.333 --> 00:34:31.250 align:middle line:84%
What about these people
that waste their money

00:34:31.250 --> 00:34:33.800 align:middle line:84%
on luxuries, expensive
cars and things?

00:34:33.800 --> 00:34:34.340 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:34:34.340 --> 00:34:36.620 align:middle line:84%
I'd be content with
a cheap rundown car.

00:34:36.620 --> 00:34:38.870 align:middle line:84%
Why should someone buy a
Ferrari when most of us

00:34:38.870 --> 00:34:40.340 align:middle line:90%
can't afford an old Kia?

00:34:40.340 --> 00:34:44.530 align:middle line:84%
Does anyone recall
Locke's answer to this?

00:34:44.530 --> 00:34:47.810 align:middle line:84%
Perhaps, once again,
we are moving too fast.

00:34:47.810 --> 00:34:51.239 align:middle line:90%
Let's go step by step.

00:34:51.239 --> 00:34:55.239 align:middle line:84%
What is the state
of nature like?

00:34:55.239 --> 00:34:55.810 align:middle line:90%
Alvis.

00:34:55.810 --> 00:34:58.810 align:middle line:84%
In addition to freedom and
equality, there is a law.

00:34:58.810 --> 00:34:59.320 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:34:59.320 --> 00:35:01.030 align:middle line:90%
What else?

00:35:01.030 --> 00:35:02.290 align:middle line:90%
There is punishment.

00:35:02.290 --> 00:35:05.590 align:middle line:84%
In paragraph seven, he
says everyone has a right

00:35:05.590 --> 00:35:08.830 align:middle line:84%
to punish the transgressors
of that law to such a degree

00:35:08.830 --> 00:35:10.720 align:middle line:90%
as may hinder its violation.

00:35:10.720 --> 00:35:11.980 align:middle line:90%
Very good, Sophie.

00:35:11.980 --> 00:35:15.100 align:middle line:84%
I think we will see that
our objection to bias

00:35:15.100 --> 00:35:18.130 align:middle line:84%
is itself based on a sense
that there is a law of nature.

00:35:18.130 --> 00:35:20.780 align:middle line:84%
How does Locke characterize
this statement?

00:35:20.780 --> 00:35:25.750 align:middle line:84%
He says in paragraph nine that
this will seem a very strange

00:35:25.750 --> 00:35:27.880 align:middle line:90%
doctrine to some.

00:35:27.880 --> 00:35:28.780 align:middle line:90%
Excellent.

00:35:28.780 --> 00:35:32.140 align:middle line:90%
Does it seem strange to you?

00:35:32.140 --> 00:35:35.350 align:middle line:84%
Well, not any more strange
than the state of nature.

00:35:35.350 --> 00:35:37.420 align:middle line:84%
Why is the state
of nature strange?

00:35:37.420 --> 00:35:40.720 align:middle line:84%
So nature is what you
experience when you go on a hike

00:35:40.720 --> 00:35:42.530 align:middle line:90%
or go camping.

00:35:42.530 --> 00:35:45.670 align:middle line:84%
What Locke is describing
never really existed.

00:35:45.670 --> 00:35:48.080 align:middle line:84%
What do you think
existed in its place?

00:35:48.080 --> 00:35:50.020 align:middle line:84%
So humans evolved
from humanoids,

00:35:50.020 --> 00:35:51.670 align:middle line:90%
and they lived in tribes.

00:35:51.670 --> 00:35:53.230 align:middle line:90%
They weren't by themselves.

00:35:53.230 --> 00:35:54.070 align:middle line:90%
Right, Patch.

00:35:54.070 --> 00:35:57.640 align:middle line:84%
But what is the basis of the
rule of the tribe leader?

00:35:57.640 --> 00:35:58.600 align:middle line:90%
Power.

00:35:58.600 --> 00:36:01.300 align:middle line:84%
But isn't this exactly what
Locke said at the beginning?

00:36:01.300 --> 00:36:03.640 align:middle line:84%
We can have tribal
rule based on power,

00:36:03.640 --> 00:36:06.520 align:middle line:84%
or we can have an alternative,
which Locke provides.

00:36:06.520 --> 00:36:10.300 align:middle line:84%
Besides, don't you think
human beings' natural state is

00:36:10.300 --> 00:36:13.240 align:middle line:90%
freedom and equality?

00:36:13.240 --> 00:36:14.230 align:middle line:90%
That all makes sense.

00:36:14.230 --> 00:36:18.280 align:middle line:84%
But then why does Locke call
it a very strange doctrine?

00:36:18.280 --> 00:36:19.510 align:middle line:90%
To some.

00:36:19.510 --> 00:36:22.300 align:middle line:84%
See, I think that will
find that what is required

00:36:22.300 --> 00:36:26.140 align:middle line:84%
is not so much a new doctrine,
say a learned latent structure

00:36:26.140 --> 00:36:30.010 align:middle line:84%
for algorithmic bias mitigation
or domain adaptation,

00:36:30.010 --> 00:36:33.010 align:middle line:84%
but a reflection
on what doctrine

00:36:33.010 --> 00:36:35.830 align:middle line:84%
some find strange
and others do not.

00:36:35.830 --> 00:36:40.270 align:middle line:84%
What's strange to us actually
contradicts our own biases.

00:36:40.270 --> 00:36:44.050 align:middle line:84%
Psychologically, we tend
to resist those things

00:36:44.050 --> 00:36:47.960 align:middle line:84%
because they point out
our biases to ourselves.

00:36:47.960 --> 00:36:50.290 align:middle line:90%
Oh, I see.

00:36:50.290 --> 00:36:55.180 align:middle line:84%
What we find strange is an
indication of our own bias.

00:36:55.180 --> 00:36:57.310 align:middle line:90%
That's what Locke is saying.

00:36:57.310 --> 00:36:58.960 align:middle line:90%
Thank you, Professor Knowles.

00:36:58.960 --> 00:37:01.240 align:middle line:84%
And I realize that he
defends this doctrine

00:37:01.240 --> 00:37:03.790 align:middle line:84%
by arguing that governments
can punish foreigners

00:37:03.790 --> 00:37:06.490 align:middle line:84%
who break a law,
which is related

00:37:06.490 --> 00:37:09.430 align:middle line:84%
to the example in
paragraph 14, when he says

00:37:09.430 --> 00:37:14.230 align:middle line:84%
that by the state of nature
exists today, since all princes

00:37:14.230 --> 00:37:16.210 align:middle line:84%
and rulers of
independent governments

00:37:16.210 --> 00:37:19.030 align:middle line:84%
all through the world
are in a state of nature.

00:37:19.030 --> 00:37:22.570 align:middle line:84%
Very good, and then he
brings in the next paragraph

00:37:22.570 --> 00:37:24.700 align:middle line:84%
the weight of the writings
of Hooker, or shall

00:37:24.700 --> 00:37:26.770 align:middle line:90%
we say the judicious Hooker.

00:37:26.770 --> 00:37:30.290 align:middle line:84%
And he ends the chapter
with the idea of consent.

00:37:30.290 --> 00:37:34.845 align:middle line:84%
So political society is made
legitimate only by consent.

00:37:34.845 --> 00:37:36.220 align:middle line:84%
Well, since we
are short on time,

00:37:36.220 --> 00:37:39.010 align:middle line:84%
I'll have to move on to
chapter five on property.

00:37:39.010 --> 00:37:40.690 align:middle line:84%
But explore when
you can what he says

00:37:40.690 --> 00:37:44.200 align:middle line:84%
about the state of war and
slavery in the Skip chapters.

00:37:44.200 --> 00:37:46.300 align:middle line:84%
We will also come back
to other subtleties,

00:37:46.300 --> 00:37:48.580 align:middle line:84%
like why he calls the
doctrine by everyone

00:37:48.580 --> 00:37:50.710 align:middle line:84%
in the state of nature
has executive power

00:37:50.710 --> 00:37:53.410 align:middle line:84%
this strange doctrine
in paragraph 13

00:37:53.410 --> 00:37:54.880 align:middle line:84%
and, for that
matter, why there is

00:37:54.880 --> 00:37:59.440 align:middle line:84%
no authority in the state of
nature besides each individual.

00:37:59.440 --> 00:38:03.810 align:middle line:84%
So what are his key
arguments on property?

00:38:03.810 --> 00:38:05.280 align:middle line:90%
Alyssa.

00:38:05.280 --> 00:38:09.300 align:middle line:84%
One is that each of us has a
property in our own person.

00:38:09.300 --> 00:38:11.310 align:middle line:90%
That's in paragraph 27.

00:38:11.310 --> 00:38:11.910 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:38:11.910 --> 00:38:15.690 align:middle line:84%
And later in paragraph 27, that
we make things our property

00:38:15.690 --> 00:38:18.090 align:middle line:84%
by mixing our labor
with things from nature.

00:38:18.090 --> 00:38:18.850 align:middle line:90%
Very good.

00:38:18.850 --> 00:38:19.350 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:38:19.350 --> 00:38:20.860 align:middle line:90%
I like the acorn example.

00:38:20.860 --> 00:38:23.910 align:middle line:84%
By picking up an
acorn, we make it ours.

00:38:23.910 --> 00:38:25.500 align:middle line:84%
I guess that applies
to fruit, too.

00:38:25.500 --> 00:38:27.330 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:38:27.330 --> 00:38:31.410 align:middle line:84%
And he says in paragraph
26 that the Earth is ours

00:38:31.410 --> 00:38:33.510 align:middle line:84%
for the support and
comfort of our being

00:38:33.510 --> 00:38:35.790 align:middle line:84%
and that we're given
reason to use it well.

00:38:35.790 --> 00:38:37.950 align:middle line:84%
That brings me back to
my point from before.

00:38:37.950 --> 00:38:40.140 align:middle line:84%
Someone isn't using it
well if they're rich

00:38:40.140 --> 00:38:41.250 align:middle line:90%
and they hoard things.

00:38:41.250 --> 00:38:43.260 align:middle line:84%
Well, you can only
hoard so much.

00:38:43.260 --> 00:38:44.910 align:middle line:90%
Like, who needs 10 Ferraris?

00:38:44.910 --> 00:38:46.440 align:middle line:90%
I do.

00:38:46.440 --> 00:38:47.580 align:middle line:90%
Ha, ha.

00:38:47.580 --> 00:38:49.080 align:middle line:90%
Ha, ha, yourself.

00:38:49.080 --> 00:38:51.540 align:middle line:84%
The point is that you cannot
hoard because your things will

00:38:51.540 --> 00:38:52.320 align:middle line:90%
spoil.

00:38:52.320 --> 00:38:53.040 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:38:53.040 --> 00:38:54.900 align:middle line:84%
That is why he talks
about money somewhere.

00:38:54.900 --> 00:38:57.060 align:middle line:90%
Money doesn't go bad.

00:38:57.060 --> 00:38:59.220 align:middle line:90%
Yes, in paragraph 36.

00:38:59.220 --> 00:39:02.040 align:middle line:84%
But you'll see that he
says in paragraph 31

00:39:02.040 --> 00:39:04.590 align:middle line:84%
that everyone can take as
much as he wants from nature,

00:39:04.590 --> 00:39:06.300 align:middle line:90%
but there is a limitation.

00:39:06.300 --> 00:39:09.360 align:middle line:84%
What is that limitation that
prevents us from accumulating

00:39:09.360 --> 00:39:12.030 align:middle line:90%
so much that things spoil?

00:39:12.030 --> 00:39:15.300 align:middle line:84%
Well, what is also present
in nature besides things

00:39:15.300 --> 00:39:16.950 align:middle line:90%
you can take?

00:39:16.950 --> 00:39:17.760 align:middle line:90%
Oh, yes.

00:39:17.760 --> 00:39:19.140 align:middle line:90%
The law of reason.

00:39:19.140 --> 00:39:22.080 align:middle line:84%
It's stupid to hoard because
then you're just wasting stuff,

00:39:22.080 --> 00:39:23.820 align:middle line:84%
and that's against
the law of reason.

00:39:23.820 --> 00:39:27.690 align:middle line:84%
But once there is money, you
can hoard it without spoiling.

00:39:27.690 --> 00:39:30.480 align:middle line:90%
So why isn't that wrong to do?

00:39:30.480 --> 00:39:34.480 align:middle line:84%
Because you put
your labor into it?

00:39:34.480 --> 00:39:36.360 align:middle line:90%
But what if you're just lucky?

00:39:36.360 --> 00:39:39.190 align:middle line:90%
Or what if you inherited it?

00:39:39.190 --> 00:39:41.370 align:middle line:90%
OK if I answer this one?

00:39:41.370 --> 00:39:45.300 align:middle line:84%
It's because someone else
put their labor into it,

00:39:45.300 --> 00:39:50.070 align:middle line:84%
therefore they can give
however they choose.

00:39:50.070 --> 00:39:50.790 align:middle line:90%
Excellent.

00:39:50.790 --> 00:39:51.960 align:middle line:90%
Thanks.

00:39:51.960 --> 00:39:54.810 align:middle line:84%
Do you recall how much
Locke says labor increases

00:39:54.810 --> 00:39:58.020 align:middle line:90%
the value of what is in nature?

00:39:58.020 --> 00:39:59.710 align:middle line:90%
100 times in paragraph 40.

00:39:59.710 --> 00:40:02.100 align:middle line:90%
1,000 times in paragraph 43.

00:40:02.100 --> 00:40:05.100 align:middle line:84%
Both 10 and 100 times
in paragraph 37.

00:40:05.100 --> 00:40:07.230 align:middle line:84%
And since we mentioned
paragraph 40,

00:40:07.230 --> 00:40:09.060 align:middle line:84%
we should point out
his famous statement

00:40:09.060 --> 00:40:10.590 align:middle line:90%
in the subsequent one.

00:40:10.590 --> 00:40:13.380 align:middle line:84%
A king of a large and
fruitful territory in America

00:40:13.380 --> 00:40:17.280 align:middle line:84%
feeds, lodges, and is clad worse
than a day laborer in England.

00:40:17.280 --> 00:40:19.890 align:middle line:84%
So whatever the
factor of increase is,

00:40:19.890 --> 00:40:23.250 align:middle line:84%
the point is not just that
labor is almost all the value

00:40:23.250 --> 00:40:26.160 align:middle line:84%
in property but that
by protecting property

00:40:26.160 --> 00:40:28.800 align:middle line:84%
we will all benefit
with many good things.

00:40:28.800 --> 00:40:30.330 align:middle line:90%
Yes, like this university.

00:40:30.330 --> 00:40:31.680 align:middle line:90%
[CHUCKLES]

00:40:31.680 --> 00:40:32.580 align:middle line:90%
Exactly.

00:40:32.580 --> 00:40:35.250 align:middle line:84%
I still don't think it's fair
that some people have so much

00:40:35.250 --> 00:40:36.910 align:middle line:90%
more than others.

00:40:36.910 --> 00:40:39.030 align:middle line:90%
But we all benefit.

00:40:39.030 --> 00:40:41.100 align:middle line:84%
Besides, if it weren't
for this system,

00:40:41.100 --> 00:40:43.560 align:middle line:84%
you wouldn't even be able
to get that broken-down Kia

00:40:43.560 --> 00:40:44.790 align:middle line:90%
that you dream of.

00:40:44.790 --> 00:40:45.300 align:middle line:90%
Oh.

00:40:45.300 --> 00:40:46.010 align:middle line:90%
Ha, ha.

00:40:46.010 --> 00:40:49.140 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:40:49.140 --> 00:40:51.510 align:middle line:84%
But even if the pie
gets bigger and bigger,

00:40:51.510 --> 00:40:53.280 align:middle line:84%
some people will
still control others

00:40:53.280 --> 00:40:54.850 align:middle line:84%
because they have
a bigger piece,

00:40:54.850 --> 00:40:58.320 align:middle line:90%
making those people unhappy.

00:40:58.320 --> 00:41:00.540 align:middle line:90%
Or do you mean less free?

00:41:00.540 --> 00:41:04.470 align:middle line:84%
If so, it seems that AI doesn't
solve the problem at all.

00:41:04.470 --> 00:41:07.020 align:middle line:84%
Of course, some people
do think that it can,

00:41:07.020 --> 00:41:08.940 align:middle line:84%
that mathematization
of all things

00:41:08.940 --> 00:41:11.880 align:middle line:84%
can lead to a sort
of techno utopia.

00:41:11.880 --> 00:41:13.840 align:middle line:90%
Locke is giving us a choice.

00:41:13.840 --> 00:41:17.460 align:middle line:84%
We can be controlled by
algorithms designed by smart

00:41:17.460 --> 00:41:21.270 align:middle line:84%
but unworldly people working
long hours in some basement--

00:41:21.270 --> 00:41:23.520 align:middle line:90%
or let's call it a cave--

00:41:23.520 --> 00:41:26.160 align:middle line:84%
or we can be allowed
our own choice.

00:41:26.160 --> 00:41:28.140 align:middle line:84%
Of course, that choice
should be the product

00:41:28.140 --> 00:41:31.350 align:middle line:84%
of thoughtful reflection,
particularly on our own biases.

00:41:31.350 --> 00:41:35.500 align:middle line:84%
But AI could give more
freedom, couldn't it?

00:41:35.500 --> 00:41:36.660 align:middle line:90%
I see what you're saying.

00:41:36.660 --> 00:41:39.300 align:middle line:84%
Certainly, it can bring
benefits in health care

00:41:39.300 --> 00:41:42.720 align:middle line:84%
and transportation and make
many things more efficient.

00:41:42.720 --> 00:41:45.180 align:middle line:84%
But intrinsically it
can't solve the problem

00:41:45.180 --> 00:41:47.910 align:middle line:90%
of bias even in itself.

00:41:47.910 --> 00:41:49.620 align:middle line:90%
Actually, it's even worse.

00:41:49.620 --> 00:41:52.620 align:middle line:84%
Those who develop and
therefore control AI

00:41:52.620 --> 00:41:56.004 align:middle line:84%
will build AI systems biased
for their own benefit.

00:41:56.004 --> 00:41:56.504 align:middle line:90%
Hmm.

00:41:56.504 --> 00:41:57.480 align:middle line:90%
Hmm.

00:41:57.480 --> 00:41:59.910 align:middle line:84%
Having said that, I
realized that we somehow

00:41:59.910 --> 00:42:02.940 align:middle line:84%
seem to have gotten
distracted from understanding

00:42:02.940 --> 00:42:07.500 align:middle line:84%
how natural right can help
us to address bias in AI.

00:42:07.500 --> 00:42:10.740 align:middle line:84%
And we only have a few
minutes of class left.

00:42:10.740 --> 00:42:13.720 align:middle line:84%
I was wondering when you
were going to realize that.

00:42:13.720 --> 00:42:16.620 align:middle line:84%
Well, you convinced
us that all AI systems

00:42:16.620 --> 00:42:18.120 align:middle line:84%
will be biased in
some dimensions

00:42:18.120 --> 00:42:20.050 align:middle line:90%
no matter what you do.

00:42:20.050 --> 00:42:23.580 align:middle line:84%
But then you also
convinced us that property

00:42:23.580 --> 00:42:25.600 align:middle line:90%
should be respected.

00:42:25.600 --> 00:42:28.650 align:middle line:90%
So, what do we do about bias?

00:42:28.650 --> 00:42:29.160 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:42:29.160 --> 00:42:31.320 align:middle line:84%
And how should we
punish people who write

00:42:31.320 --> 00:42:33.540 align:middle line:90%
unacceptably biased AI systems?

00:42:33.540 --> 00:42:37.990 align:middle line:84%
Or reward them for making
acceptably biased AI systems.

00:42:37.990 --> 00:42:39.630 align:middle line:90%
I mean unbiased AI systems.

00:42:39.630 --> 00:42:41.100 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:42:41.100 --> 00:42:41.610 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:42:41.610 --> 00:42:43.260 align:middle line:84%
We can't reward
or punish people,

00:42:43.260 --> 00:42:45.690 align:middle line:90%
according to Locke's system.

00:42:45.690 --> 00:42:48.330 align:middle line:84%
On the contrary,
we can reward them

00:42:48.330 --> 00:42:50.490 align:middle line:84%
by buying their
products if we like them

00:42:50.490 --> 00:42:52.650 align:middle line:84%
or punish them by not
buying their products

00:42:52.650 --> 00:42:54.070 align:middle line:90%
if we don't like them.

00:42:54.070 --> 00:42:56.310 align:middle line:90%
So transparency is the key?

00:42:56.310 --> 00:42:59.850 align:middle line:84%
Yes, if people are willing
to pay for transparency.

00:42:59.850 --> 00:43:02.910 align:middle line:84%
You see, the law of
nature addresses that too.

00:43:02.910 --> 00:43:04.860 align:middle line:84%
And in most
contemporary approaches,

00:43:04.860 --> 00:43:06.810 align:middle line:84%
there is an
assumption, one which

00:43:06.810 --> 00:43:08.730 align:middle line:84%
we have been
addressing today too,

00:43:08.730 --> 00:43:11.550 align:middle line:84%
that bias is bad and
should be eradicated

00:43:11.550 --> 00:43:13.920 align:middle line:90%
by technical mathematical means.

00:43:13.920 --> 00:43:17.220 align:middle line:84%
We now understand that a deeper
reflection on the law of nature

00:43:17.220 --> 00:43:20.070 align:middle line:84%
and the law of reason is
needed, but we engineers

00:43:20.070 --> 00:43:22.890 align:middle line:84%
needed to see the limitations
of the mathematical or computer

00:43:22.890 --> 00:43:25.200 align:middle line:84%
science approach
first before we turn

00:43:25.200 --> 00:43:26.830 align:middle line:90%
to a broader or deeper one.

00:43:26.830 --> 00:43:27.330 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:43:27.330 --> 00:43:30.870 align:middle line:84%
I think I understand
this now, but it still

00:43:30.870 --> 00:43:32.760 align:middle line:90%
hasn't changed one thing.

00:43:32.760 --> 00:43:34.020 align:middle line:90%
I want a Ferrari.

00:43:34.020 --> 00:43:36.060 align:middle line:90%
[SCOFFING, CHUCKLING]

00:43:36.060 --> 00:43:40.410 align:middle line:84%
The point is under the Lockean
system we are all better off--

00:43:40.410 --> 00:43:42.000 align:middle line:90%
happier, we could say--

00:43:42.000 --> 00:43:44.340 align:middle line:84%
because algorithms
with unacceptable

00:43:44.340 --> 00:43:47.760 align:middle line:84%
bias will go away because
no one will buy them.

00:43:47.760 --> 00:43:50.580 align:middle line:90%
You're starting to understand.

00:43:50.580 --> 00:43:53.100 align:middle line:84%
Earlier, you mentioned
utilitarianism,

00:43:53.100 --> 00:43:56.260 align:middle line:84%
and you showed not only
that AI is tied up in it

00:43:56.260 --> 00:43:59.370 align:middle line:84%
but that it doesn't treat
the human things well.

00:43:59.370 --> 00:44:01.140 align:middle line:84%
But what I didn't
really understand

00:44:01.140 --> 00:44:04.440 align:middle line:84%
is that we still need to
make utilitarian choices,

00:44:04.440 --> 00:44:06.990 align:middle line:90%
like allocation of resources.

00:44:06.990 --> 00:44:10.410 align:middle line:84%
Could you show us how to
think about that necessity

00:44:10.410 --> 00:44:13.110 align:middle line:84%
within the realm
of human things?

00:44:13.110 --> 00:44:14.460 align:middle line:90%
Yes, I can.

00:44:14.460 --> 00:44:17.730 align:middle line:84%
We did say that utilitarianism
as a form of thought

00:44:17.730 --> 00:44:20.130 align:middle line:84%
tends to give an incomplete
account of human concerns

00:44:20.130 --> 00:44:21.930 align:middle line:84%
and passions, no
matter how much we

00:44:21.930 --> 00:44:24.180 align:middle line:84%
strive to make the
mathematical model complete.

00:44:24.180 --> 00:44:27.600 align:middle line:84%
But we did indicate that
engineering is utilitarian

00:44:27.600 --> 00:44:31.110 align:middle line:84%
and that it always strives
to optimize some function.

00:44:31.110 --> 00:44:33.630 align:middle line:84%
How we should think about
that for human benefit

00:44:33.630 --> 00:44:36.160 align:middle line:84%
would be the subject
for next class.

00:44:36.160 --> 00:44:36.660 align:middle line:90%
[BELL RINGS]

00:44:36.660 --> 00:44:40.010 align:middle line:90%
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:44:40.010 --> 00:45:45.000 align:middle line:90%